## Solution

Define $Q(x) = x \cdot P(x) - 1$.

Since $P(x)$ has degree 1996, $Q(x)$ has degree 1997. For each $n = 1, 2, \ldots, 1997$:

$$Q(n) = n \cdot P(n) - 1 = n \cdot \frac{1}{n} - 1 = 0.$$

So $Q(x)$ has roots $1, 2, \ldots, 1997$, giving

$$Q(x) = c\,(x-1)(x-2)\cdots(x-1997)$$

for some constant $c$ equal to the leading coefficient of $Q$, which is the leading coefficient of $P$.

**Determining $c$.** Evaluate at $x = 0$:

$$Q(0) = 0 \cdot P(0) - 1 = -1,$$

$$Q(0) = c\,(-1)(-2)\cdots(-1997) = c\,(-1)^{1997}\cdot 1997! = -c\cdot 1997!.$$

So $-1 = -c\cdot 1997!$, hence $c = \dfrac{1}{1997!}$.

**Evaluating $P(1998)$.** 

$$Q(1998) = 1998\cdot P(1998) - 1 = c\,(1997)(1996)\cdots(1) = c\cdot 1997! = \frac{1}{1997!}\cdot 1997! = 1.$$

Therefore

$$1998\cdot P(1998) - 1 = 1 \implies 1998\cdot P(1998) = 2 \implies P(1998) = \frac{2}{1998} = \frac{1}{999}.$$

$$\boxed{P(1998) = \dfrac{1}{999}}$$

